"Alexa, ask Encore to search for Dune" opens a list of what matches on the TV, for you to pick from with the remote, where "play Dune" plays the best match straight away.

## Saying it

- "search for Dune", "search Kodi for Dune", "global search for Dune": anything that matches.
- "search for the film Dune", "search Kodi for the movie Dune": films only.
- "search for the show Severance", "search Kodi for the TV show Severance": TV shows only.
- "search for the artist Queen", "search for the album Innuendo": music only.

Each language says it its own way; see the [voice commands]({{base}}/voice-commands/) for yours. The film, show and music forms only matter when searching the library: Global Search and a skin's own search look through everything. Alexa sometimes hears one as a plain search with the kind still in it, such as a search for "the film Dune"; Encore takes the kind off and searches as you meant, so Kodi is never asked to find the word "film".

## Ways to search

Choose how Encore searches with **Search** under **Settings** → **Features** ([`alexa.search_mode`]({{base}}/reference/configuration/#alexasearch_mode)). Each Kodi instance can override it with **Search** in its dialog, or `search_mode` for an instance declared in config.

- **Show it in the library** (`library`, the default) shows the matching films, TV shows, music videos, artists or albums in Kodi's own library view, drawn by your skin like any other. It needs no add-ons.
- **Global Search** (`global_search`) runs Kodi's [Global Search](https://kodi.wiki/view/Add-on:Global_Search) add-on, which looks through the library and its add-ons at once. Global Search draws its window from the skin's `script-globalsearch.xml`; a skin that doesn't ship one, such as Arctic Fuse 3, gets Global Search's own default look.
- **The skin's own search** (`skin`) opens the skin's own search window with what you said filled in, for the [skins listed below](#skins-with-their-own-search). On a skin that draws Global Search's window itself, its search is Global Search.

When Global Search or the skin's search can't run, because the add-on isn't installed, the skin isn't one Encore knows, or a step fails, the search shows the library instead, and the [request log]({{base}}/guides/request-log/) entry's note says why, e.g. `skin search: skin.copacetic has no profile; showed the library`.

## The library

Encore matches what you said against the library as it does for "play", and opens the list of the kind whose title matched best, filtered to the titles that contain what you said: "search for star" lists every film with "star" in its title when a film matched best. Kodi's filter matches inside words too, so a show called *The Bastard Son* turns up for "star".

When titles of several kinds match equally well, the one whose title is exactly what you said wins, then films, TV shows, music videos, artists and albums in that order, so a film beats its soundtrack. A film and a TV show with exactly the same name, such as *Watchmen*, can't be told apart: Alexa asks "Do you want the film or the TV show Watchmen?", and "the film", "the show", "the first one" or "the second one" answers. Saying which in the first place, as in "search for the show Watchmen", doesn't ask.

Kinds the skill was set up without (see [content]({{base}}/reference/configuration/#alexacontent)) aren't searched. When nothing matches, Alexa says so.

## Skin search

Encore asks Kodi which skin it's running with every search, so you can change skins at any time without telling Encore. The instance dialog shows the skin Kodi is running now and what a search does on it, for example *Arctic Fuse 3: skin search supported*, *Aeon Nox: SiLVO: its search is Global Search, which it skins* or *Copacetic: no profile, so searches show the library*.

In skin mode, a search:

1. reads the active skin from Kodi (`lookandfeel.skin`);
2. looks the skin's add-on ID up in the tables below, exactly;
3. checks that the add-ons the skin's search needs are installed and enabled;
4. opens the skin's search window, waits for it to appear, and fills in what you said.

JSON-RPC can't run Kodi's builtin commands, and `GUI.ActivateWindow` only opens Kodi's named windows, not a skin's custom ones, so Encore drives the skin through an add-on on the Kodi host: [Skin Variables](https://github.com/jurialmunkey/script.skinvariables) (`script.skinvariables`), which the Arctic skins below already depend on, or the skin's own helper add-on.

Skin Variables is both a script and a plugin, and Kodi's `Addons.ExecuteAddon` opens such an add-on as its plugin rather than running its script. So Encore lists the plugin's `run_executebuiltin` route with `Files.GetDirectory`, which opens no window, pointing it at `shortcuts/builtins/skinvariables-closeosd.json`: a rules file these skins ship that closes any on-screen display and then runs the command it's given, such as `ReplaceWindow(1105)`. Where it can, Encore then focuses the search box that way and types what you said itself, so it never passes through Kodi's builtin parser.

